We end this section with an example. Let $G$ be the graph appearing in Figure~\ref{fig:U1_ex}, which we further consider in \textsection \ref{sub:1and2_3}. The graph $G$ is minimally generated by the vertices $\alpha$, $\alpha'$, and $\beta$. Therefore, for each number field $K$, we define
	\[
		U_1(G)(K) = \{(\alpha,\alpha',\beta,c) \in \bbA^4(K) : \text{ $\alpha$ and $\alpha'$ are distinct fixed points and $\beta$ has type $2_3$ for $f_c$} \}.
	\]
By Remark~\ref{rem:redundant}, if $G'$ is the subgraph of $G$ generated by $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and if we write
	\[
		U_1(G')(K) = \{(\alpha,\beta,c) \in \bbA^3(K) : \text{ $\alpha$ has period $1$ and $\beta$ has type $2_3$ for $f_c$} \},
	\]
then the curves $U_1(G)$ and $U_1(G')$ are birational.

